WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.360
ANANTH SUNDARRAJAN: Hello,
and welcome to Next OnAir,

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.420
and welcome to our
session on managing

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.230
enterprise-scale distributed
business and operations

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.420
on Google Cloud.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.210
Before we jump into our
exciting talk today,

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:23.140
let me take a moment
to introduce ourselves.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.770
I'm Ananth Sundarrajan.

00:00:24.770 --> 00:00:28.730
I manage the Go-To-Market
function for Telco, Media,

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.240
Entertainment, and Gaming in
the Google Cloud Professional

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.390
Services.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.520
I am joined by Venk Subramanian,
Director of Engineering

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.270
at Unity, who has been
instrumental in Unity's

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.520
migration into GCP.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.360
I also have Som Roy, who heads
our professional services

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.730
scheme in the San
Francisco region.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.800
In this session,
we will walk you

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.980
through how Unity and
Google are partnering

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:58.760
on making this world
a better gaming place.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:02.000
We will walk through
Unity's migration journey

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.500
and how Unity has been
managing, scaling, and unlocking

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.020
the part of their
GCP environment,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.760
especially during our
current pandemic situation.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.780
We will also walk through a
behind-the-scenes story of how

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.010
Google Cloud account teams and
professional services teams

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.950
have been and continue
to support Unity

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.750
throughout this entire journey.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:31.770
Before we dive into the details,
for those of you who have not

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.890
heard about the Google
and Unity partnership,

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.660
here's a quick
overview from Venk.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:38.710
VENK SUBRAMANIAN: Hello.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:43.000
I'm Venk, and I'm a director
of engineering here at Unity.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.800
We are the world's
leading platform

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:48.670
for creating, operating,
interactive real-time 3D

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.530
content.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.350
Here at Unity, we believe
the world is a better

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.330
place with more creators in it.

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.550
This is our philosophy,
and it is at the heart

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.270
of everything we do.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.110
We want to empower developers
with our real-time 3D

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:06.850
technology to help turn
their dreams into reality.

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.130
Unity focuses on
giving content creators

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:14.290
a single end-to-end solution for
everything they need to create.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.930
And we do that with
best-in-class tools

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.510
and workflows that
empower content creators

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.380
to do more and operate,
enabling creators

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.720
to launch their
content and support it

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:29.410
with a full stack of
engine-agnostic services

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.690
that make things
easier for developers,

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:34.930
keep their content running,
help them optimize it,

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.270
and even monetize
and drive revenue

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.660
through in-app purchases
or serving ads.

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:42.880
To give you an
idea of our scale,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.640
3 billion apps were
downloaded last year

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:47.440
that were made with Unity.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.080
Half of all games are
powered by Unity, averaged

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:52.810
across console, PC, and mobile.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:56.230
And those games and content
from many other industries run

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:58.720
across 20 platforms,
including game

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.910
consoles, mobile phones, AR/VR
devices that Unity supports.

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.880
We give developers the
power to target and optimize

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.360
their content to reach the
widest possible audience.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.060
I also want to highlight that
our cloud services usually

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.450
operate at over 100,000
requests per second.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.240
So building a strong GCP
management foundation

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.570
was critical to our success.

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.880
ANANTH SUNDARRAJAN: To
support Unity's global scheme

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.970
and developer needs, Google
has partnered with Unity

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:31.480
for years on a broad
and significant level

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.190
across the company.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:36.310
A number of official
strategic partnerships

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:40.150
help our critical elements
of the Unity platform

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.360
and solve hard problems
for the developers.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.030
For example, Google Cloud's
partnership with Unity

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.580
puts connected games at the
fingertips of all developers.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.950
Regardless of
engine choice, they

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.370
have access to tools to
build live multiplayer

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:02.540
games more easily and without
needing to be a cloud expert.

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.210
We also provide the back-end
cloud for nearly all

00:04:05.210 --> 00:04:07.610
of Unity's cloud-based
products to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:11.360
Stadia and ARCore partnerships
offer integrated platform

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.210
support to Unity
game developers that

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:16.459
want to publish
to those platforms

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:19.279
with familiar tools
and workflows that

00:04:19.279 --> 00:04:24.830
make it easy for them to build
once and deploy anywhere.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:29.290
AdMob's partnership opens up
Unity's extensive mobile ad

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:32.080
network to Google
brand advertisers

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.310
and streamlines how publishers
with Unity mobile games

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.530
can monetize their apps.

00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:43.350
And finally, DeepMind leverages
Unity for a search platform

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:47.980
to run large-scale simulations
in virtual environments

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.560
[INAUDIBLE] AI and machine
learning and so on.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:54.250
If you go a layer deeper into
all of these partnerships,

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:59.800
a key factor driving success is
how well Google and Unity teams

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.130
collaborate on everything
from product development

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.290
and capacity planning
to technical support,

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:09.190
co-marketing campaigns
for product launches,

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:10.880
and much more.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:14.755
This mindset is especially true
of Google Cloud's partnership

00:05:14.755 --> 00:05:16.810
with Unity.

00:05:16.810 --> 00:05:19.280
In relatively short
time, we joined

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.190
to kickstart the Connected
Games initiative that I just

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:25.310
mentioned, and as
part of it, co-founded

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.770
Open Match, an open
source project created

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.360
to help game developers
solve the tricky problem

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.300
of matchmaking,
which is matching

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.980
the right players together in
a live multiplayer environment.

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.490
We were thrilled to release
the production-ready Open Match

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:46.320
1.0 earlier this summer.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.300
GCP infrastructure also helps
Unity's Multiplay, a game

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.460
server hosting platform,
scale into the cloud

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:59.580
when needed to support new game
launches and unexpected player

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:02.260
demand spikes.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:06.040
This was the case last year
when Respawn launched the battle

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.790
royale game "Apex Legends."

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:15.110
50 million gamers went online
to play in the first 24 days,

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:19.450
including 1 million
in the first 24 hours.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:23.280
Flexibility was crucial.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.820
GCP helped Multiplay
scale quickly

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:32.660
into its servers in seconds,
not hours, with zero downtime.

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:36.110
Unity Simulation and
Unity Game Simulation,

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:40.370
two exciting new cloud-based
products in open beta,

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.560
also run on Google
Cloud, allowing

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.770
Unity to accelerate
product development

00:06:45.770 --> 00:06:47.960
and upgrade both
servers at scale.

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:54.060
All this amazing work at
Google Cloud and Unity

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:57.600
have achieved so far started
with migrating Unity's

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.120
infrastructure onto GCP.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:05.560
Unity's philosophy of
democratizing game development

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:09.300
is synonymous with their vast
and distributed engineering

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:10.230
footprint.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:14.560
With key development hubs
spread across three continents,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:17.950
this posed a unique challenge
for the migrating teams

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:22.780
who work cross-border
towards a single goal.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.930
To support this geographically
distributed migration,

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.030
the professional
services team was

00:07:28.030 --> 00:07:31.420
quick to bring together
amazing individuals

00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:35.920
across the globe who ensured not
just technical presence in each

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:40.060
of Unity's development hubs to
provide technical assistance

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:44.620
and guidance, but also to
be close to Unity's team

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:48.550
and partner with the
teams during the migration

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:54.020
and also celebrate together once
the migration was completed.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.910
We completed the
entire migration

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:58.230
over the course of nine months.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.960
The key here is the
first three months

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.370
we spent on discovering,
strategizing,

00:08:04.370 --> 00:08:06.620
and planning the migration.

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.720
By the end of the
third month, we

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:12.710
had a clear understanding
of how and when

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:16.130
we wanted to migrate the
workloads to minimize

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:17.180
any disruption.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:20.000
But the journey does not
end with the completion

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:21.380
of migration.

00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:25.400
Rather, it only created a
launch pad for all of the future

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:29.270
workloads Unity was
able to bring on to GCP.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:31.600
We launched "Apex
Legends" game, which

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.570
I spoke about earlier,
in Q1 of 2019,

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:37.700
right after the migration.

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.559
And since then,
we've been working

00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:43.520
on onboarding Unity's
acquisitions such as Vivox,

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.710
deltaDNA, ChilliConnect,
and Furioos onto GCP.

00:08:48.710 --> 00:08:53.120
This increased footprint also
required careful planning

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.210
in terms of capacity
in key markets,

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:59.090
both on Google's side
and the Unity side,

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:01.850
to ensure scaling when needed.

00:09:01.850 --> 00:09:04.420
We saw the results of
this careful planning

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:09.040
when the number of gamers surged
during the past six months.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:12.480
Let's get into some of
the details of how Unity

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:16.410
has been managing this large
GCP landscape with a very

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:17.790
agile team.

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:22.470
I will let Venk walk us
through the secret sauce.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.120
VENK SUBRAMANIAN:
Last year at Next,

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:26.310
we talked about how
to effectively manage

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.410
a migration to GCP.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:29.970
This year, we'd
like to follow up

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:33.900
with what happens once
you're running in GCP.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:36.150
This next section will
help you understand

00:09:36.150 --> 00:09:41.450
how to efficiently manage a
business at scale in the cloud.

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:44.020
The first thing to
plan for is continuing

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:46.270
the momentum and
engagement you've

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:48.880
gathered moving over to GCP.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:50.740
Don't wait to finish up.

00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:52.370
Plan ahead.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:55.450
Here are some examples
of ways we did that.

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.740
We re-architected key portions
of our stack to leverage GCP

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:01.760
managed technologies.

00:10:01.760 --> 00:10:04.450
New products, like
Unity Simulations,

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:08.050
were built foundationally
to leverage GCP.

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:10.090
We also worked
closely with Google

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:14.710
to build our partnership
through joint initiatives.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:16.600
After setting the
initial direction,

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:19.600
we had a simple vision
going forward with managing

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:22.390
our high-scale
technology in GCP--

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:27.730
manage your operating expenses
like you manage your revenue.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.370
This vision was realized
in three strategic areas--

00:10:31.370 --> 00:10:34.700
GCP organization,
budget management,

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.400
and capacity planning.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:40.150
Let's dig into the first
strategic area of focus,

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:42.650
GCP organization.

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:46.760
I have found that Conway's
law provides valuable insight

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:50.330
into setting up effective
technology organizations.

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:52.970
In our context,
Conway's law states

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:55.850
that a system will mimic the
organization structure that

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:57.230
built it.

00:10:57.230 --> 00:11:00.800
We applied this principle
to enable engineering teams

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:04.520
by setting up GCP to mirror
the organizational structure

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:05.900
within Unity.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.890
For us, that meant a flat
project and folder hierarchy,

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.800
where folders lined up with
organizations and projects

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.290
lined up with product services.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:17.640
We also automated all
these capabilities

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.580
so engineering
teams were empowered

00:11:19.580 --> 00:11:22.520
to own their infrastructure,
while at the same time

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.970
following common standards,
including business-level

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:31.100
tagging to make it easy to track
spend and map it to products.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.160
We restricted and
automated production access

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:37.460
using Slack bots and
provided standard modules

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.000
that engineering teams could
use to build VMs, databases,

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.510
and more.

00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:45.620
Finally, we applied
Conway's law in reverse

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:47.900
to set up our core
infrastructure teams

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:51.920
to mirror the GCP management
systems we put in place.

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:54.890
This also included
establishing a DevOps culture

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:58.100
within each organization
so they were empowered

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.370
to own their infrastructure
and organize in the way that

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:03.020
suited their business.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:05.480
Today, we have
established successfully

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:10.790
a DevOps group in every
organization across Unity.

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:12.980
Let's move on to our
second strategic area

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:15.830
of focus, budget management.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.620
GCP provides a deep
set of tools to manage

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.320
enterprise-scale
budgets, further enhanced

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:23.330
by partnership with TAMs.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:27.300
Let's break this area
down into a few sections.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.330
The first section is spend.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:32.390
The most important thing for
you to know and understand

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:33.800
is your spend--

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.840
not just the numbers
itself, but how

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.650
it relates to products,
services, and technology.

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:43.880
GCP provides helpful tools here
for you to regularly review

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:49.300
the breakdown of where your
GCP spend goes using reports.

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.060
Once you understand
your spend, you

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:54.190
can build custom reports
for leadership visibility,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:58.590
and also to enable your teams to
own and manage their GCP spend.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:02.690
Data Studio is a
great tool here.

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:05.470
The next section is to
focus on forecasting.

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.360
This creates the
ability for you to have

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:09.940
predictable future GCP spend.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:12.250
Here are some simple guidelines.

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:16.300
Tag all your infrastructure with
business-related information.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:18.200
Formulate a unit.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:20.980
This means creating a
formula that maps your GCP

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.380
spend to your business outcomes.

00:13:23.380 --> 00:13:26.140
In our case, an obvious
one was the GCP cost

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:28.780
to serve up a single
request and establish

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:32.830
that cost as the baseline
we wanted to optimize.

00:13:32.830 --> 00:13:35.600
Once you have a unit,
create automation

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:39.430
so you can regularly attribute
GCP spend to these units.

00:13:39.430 --> 00:13:42.940
It also helps to understand your
discounts and credits in order

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:44.690
to do this.

00:13:44.690 --> 00:13:48.110
Establish a process of
doing regular forecasting.

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:51.080
And do that by integrating it
into your business planning

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:52.530
processes.

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:55.040
It also helps to have a
cross-expertise working group

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:58.700
to manage and optimize
this forecast.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:01.700
Finally, plan and commit.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:04.280
GCP provides helpful
tools to commit,

00:14:04.280 --> 00:14:07.400
including GKE committed
usage discounts, BigQuery

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.380
fixed slots, and more.

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:10.760
For you to be able
to commit, you

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.700
need to have an effective
planning methodology.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:17.450
Look to align with
your business strategy.

00:14:17.450 --> 00:14:20.930
The last strategic area of
focus to cover in managing GCP

00:14:20.930 --> 00:14:22.640
is capacity planning.

00:14:22.640 --> 00:14:25.250
This lets you plan ahead
for any cloud technologies

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:27.620
you will need to scale
into and collaborate

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:29.610
with Google ahead of time.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:31.490
Here are some key points.

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:34.100
Work with Google to
understand your region, zone,

00:14:34.100 --> 00:14:35.690
and [? SKU ?] limits.

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:39.740
Use forecasts to plan for
capacity ahead of time.

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:42.170
Communicate these forecasts
and capacity needs

00:14:42.170 --> 00:14:44.270
with Google ahead of time.

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:45.350
And iterate.

00:14:45.350 --> 00:14:46.970
Consistency and
predictability is

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:50.810
built by doing this regularly.

00:14:50.810 --> 00:14:52.820
This year, the
pandemic has become

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:56.210
a critical and disruptive
facet of our lives.

00:14:56.210 --> 00:14:58.670
As the world
quarantined, at Unity,

00:14:58.670 --> 00:15:00.890
we saw an increased
engagement with games

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:03.230
from players across the globe.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:06.530
Here are some ways
we planned for it.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:10.020
From the get-go, we focused
on staying ahead of the scale.

00:15:10.020 --> 00:15:11.570
The last thing we
wanted to do was

00:15:11.570 --> 00:15:15.440
disrupt the player experience
due to our inability to handle

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.720
traffic during these times.

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:21.320
This meant focusing on our
users and their interactions

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:25.580
with our systems and staying
ahead of potential issues.

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:29.090
Our central infrastructure teams
doubled down on their focus

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:30.470
on GCP--

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:32.960
in particular, SRE
and the engineering

00:15:32.960 --> 00:15:36.180
teams with ownership
of high-scale services.

00:15:36.180 --> 00:15:40.160
This also led to proactive
collaboration with GCP to plan

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:42.710
for upcoming scale needs.

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:46.400
Finally-- and I consider
this to be so important--

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.120
as our engineering teams focused
on handling increased scale,

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:53.750
we also doubled down on our
focus on their well-being.

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:57.290
It's easy in such cases to feel
like it's all hands on deck.

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:00.290
But it is equally important
that your engineers focus

00:16:00.290 --> 00:16:02.690
on their personal
health and well-being

00:16:02.690 --> 00:16:06.770
and that they feel supported by
their organization in doing so.

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:09.800
I encourage you to be
intentional in planning

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:11.351
for this.

00:16:11.351 --> 00:16:14.590
These philosophies on managing
enterprise scale in GCP

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:16.640
have worked well
for us at Unity.

00:16:16.640 --> 00:16:18.610
And I look forward
to hearing from you

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:20.911
on how it has helped you too.

00:16:20.911 --> 00:16:23.340
SOM ROY: Thank you, Venk,
for really giving us

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.490
a good view into
how Unity's managing

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.310
their operations on GCP.

00:16:28.310 --> 00:16:31.020
So in this last bit today
in the presentation,

00:16:31.020 --> 00:16:34.140
we are going to talk about
how, on the Google side,

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:37.530
we are enabling Unity's
success behind the scenes.

00:16:37.530 --> 00:16:40.560
And the first part of that,
what we want to talk about

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:44.700
is the shared goal is that the
Google team and Unity had laid

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:48.360
out at the beginning of
our engagement together

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:51.465
and how we have continued
it through the years.

00:16:51.465 --> 00:16:54.420
You know, the Google PSO
team and the broader Customer

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.840
Experience Org., including
our CSM team and support team,

00:16:57.840 --> 00:17:00.510
and Unity had taken a
#SharedGoals approach

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:04.650
to this relationship, which has
ultimately resulted in shared

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:08.130
success and has also
helped Unity in its bigger

00:17:08.130 --> 00:17:11.220
mission of empowering
creators across industries

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:12.450
and around the world.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:15.780
Setting up this shared goal
was really, really important

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:19.589
because that led to the creation
of our joint success framework

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:24.119
that I want to talk about,
because this was really, really

00:17:24.119 --> 00:17:26.450
important, again, to
enable and making sure

00:17:26.450 --> 00:17:28.590
Unity is successful on GCP.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:31.110
The first pillar of the
joint success framework

00:17:31.110 --> 00:17:33.510
is the deep engineering
partnership.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:35.505
Unity engineering and
Google engineering

00:17:35.505 --> 00:17:39.240
are tied to the hip and not--

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:42.460
engineering interactions on
a daily basis is the norm.

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:46.440
And that's how we have managed
for the last two years, which

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:51.390
ultimately leads to Unity
shaping a lot of the Google

00:17:51.390 --> 00:17:53.332
products that--

00:17:53.332 --> 00:17:55.950
and include really
strongly influencing

00:17:55.950 --> 00:17:58.161
our product roadmap.

00:17:58.161 --> 00:18:00.150
Unity engineering and
Google engineering,

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:03.840
again, partner on open
source contribution as well.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.220
Open Match is one of the
well-known open source

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.480
contributions that
we have had jointly.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:12.640
There's many more that
we are working on.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:16.180
And then finally, Ananth talked
about the "Apex Legend" launch.

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:18.510
A launch like that
would not be successful

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:21.330
if we do not do proactive
capacity planning

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:23.660
for each workload
that we're onboarding

00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:25.560
and each launches
that are happening.

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.710
And proactive capacity planning,
understanding the requirement,

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:31.020
making sure those
regions and the zones--

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:32.670
like, we are planning
to the capacity,

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:35.370
has really, really
been helpful for Unity

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:36.850
to scale up on the platform.

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:39.180
So that's all on the deep
engineering partnership

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:42.330
that we have across these
different attributes.

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:46.230
Next part of the pillar is the
professional services and Unity

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:46.820
interlock.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:50.940
The PSO team has been working as
an extension of the Unity team

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:52.090
for the past two years.

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:55.560
What that means is some of the
team members globally working

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:58.388
on Unity account has actually
spent more time at Unity

00:18:58.388 --> 00:18:59.430
than they have at Google.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:03.667
That's how close we
are to the Unity team.

00:19:03.667 --> 00:19:06.000
And that's how PSO is working
with them for the last two

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.260
years.

00:19:07.260 --> 00:19:10.830
Secondly, a lot of our
internal developer tools

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:15.070
has already integrated with the
developer workflow for Unity,

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:18.150
which means that we
have been able to foster

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:20.880
a lot of collaboration
between the two teams

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:23.800
and the TAMs have
been primarily really,

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:26.390
really important to
enable that collaboration.

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:29.370
A third piece is what
the TAMs are enabling

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:33.570
is many, many multiple product
management sessions for each

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:36.280
of the existing products and
what's coming down the pipe.

00:19:36.280 --> 00:19:38.280
I know you saw in the
deep engineering partition

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:42.960
where I talked about how Unity's
shaping our product roadmap.

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:45.470
That's been enabled through
these product sessions, which

00:19:45.470 --> 00:19:49.390
the TAMs are driving and
doing each and every quarter.

00:19:49.390 --> 00:19:52.320
And finally, the other thing
that the TAMs really help

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:55.260
is the war room setup for each
of these critical launches.

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:57.450
Like, when a new game
launches-- and again,

00:19:57.450 --> 00:19:58.980
talking to the "Apex
Legend" launch,

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:02.810
they had 50 million users in 24
days that Ananth talked about.

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:06.630
Like, to manage that scale,
we need to have war room.

00:20:06.630 --> 00:20:08.380
Things always might go wrong.

00:20:08.380 --> 00:20:11.550
So setting up this war room
for every critical launch

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.450
is really, really important.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:15.960
The third pillar of
the success framework

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:18.040
is enablement and learning.

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.850
That means GCP training
is well integrated

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:24.670
into the curriculum of Unity's
internal learning path.

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:28.410
That means each and every
engineer who touches GCP has

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:31.200
to get upscaled on GCP.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:34.680
And I'll talk about what we are
doing from our side to enable

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.780
further training within Unity.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.720
Right now, we have
about 100 engineers

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:41.220
from Unity already
taking multiple classes.

00:20:41.220 --> 00:20:44.380
And we're aiming to almost
double that number this year.

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:47.190
And then finally, Unity
and Google are always

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:49.500
partnering on evangelism event.

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:52.200
Like, for example, this
is one of these sessions.

00:20:52.200 --> 00:20:54.380
But then also, we
have participated

00:20:54.380 --> 00:20:58.050
at Unity's event, which is
Unite, and jointly presented.

00:20:58.050 --> 00:21:01.860
So overall, we are always
kind of sharing our story

00:21:01.860 --> 00:21:05.790
with the broader world through
separate evangelism events.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:08.850
And then finally, the last
pillar of the joint success

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:11.145
framework is the
strong exec sponsorship

00:21:11.145 --> 00:21:13.710
that we have from both sides.

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:17.920
We have executive-level
leadership councils

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:20.690
that meet annually
or biannually to talk

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:22.850
through how things are
going and reviewing

00:21:22.850 --> 00:21:24.230
the broader partnership.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:26.090
We have quarterly
technical reviews

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:28.340
each quarter to assess
the partnership goals

00:21:28.340 --> 00:21:30.500
and also plan to work
out the priorities

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:32.210
for the next coming quarter.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:35.120
And then more tactically, there
is weekly program management

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:37.700
sync for strategic
alignment and making sure

00:21:37.700 --> 00:21:39.500
what we are doing
on a weekly basis

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:42.210
is aligned to what Unity
needs at the moment.

00:21:42.210 --> 00:21:44.840
So all of this put together
creates the joint success

00:21:44.840 --> 00:21:47.087
framework that has--

00:21:47.087 --> 00:21:48.920
[INAUDIBLE] talks about
the engagement model

00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:52.370
we have with Unity that we have
taken over the last few years

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:56.760
as Unity has scaled, migrated,
and scaled up on the platform.

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:00.800
Next, I'm going to
talk about particularly

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:03.260
how Customer Experience Org.

00:22:03.260 --> 00:22:07.490
within Google has been helping
Unity begin behind the scenes,

00:22:07.490 --> 00:22:11.420
enabling all of the
things that you saw.

00:22:11.420 --> 00:22:13.000
I talked a little bit on TAMs.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:16.490
The TAMs are working on the
long-term success and advocacy

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:19.220
and bringing the product
expertise and best practices

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:20.540
to Unity team.

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:22.940
We have three TAMs
aligned globally right now

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:25.520
who are supporting
Unity day in and day out

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:27.470
as they scale up
on the platform.

00:22:27.470 --> 00:22:29.590
Second point, and I'm
going to double-click

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:31.040
on this in the
next section, which

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:33.440
is foundation and training,
which is [INAUDIBLE],,

00:22:33.440 --> 00:22:37.490
again, enabling Unity on GCP
just from a scale perspective.

00:22:37.490 --> 00:22:39.410
Third is a very
important piece, which

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:41.840
is the Google Customer
Reliability Engineering

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:42.590
Program.

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:44.750
We call it Google CRE Program.

00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:48.040
And the CREs are
basically ex-Google SREs

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:49.520
who are customer-adjacent.

00:22:49.520 --> 00:22:52.880
And our CREs have been
partnering very closely

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:56.930
with Unity's SRE team to design
for reliability, operational

00:22:56.930 --> 00:22:59.330
readiness around
particular applications

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:01.160
that we are
onboarding, as well as

00:23:01.160 --> 00:23:04.640
making sure we are configuring
platform signals so we are

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:08.630
proactively getting alerts when
potentially something might

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:10.380
go wrong in the future.

00:23:10.380 --> 00:23:12.410
This has been a very,
very critical element

00:23:12.410 --> 00:23:14.840
of making sure Unity
has a great customer

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.340
experience on the
the platform, just

00:23:16.340 --> 00:23:18.540
being proactive
about these things.

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:21.090
And then lastly, again, I
touched upon this a little bit,

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:24.260
which is the go-live and
the critical event support.

00:23:24.260 --> 00:23:26.180
Every time we are
setting up war room,

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:28.910
making sure we are planning
the capacity, making sure

00:23:28.910 --> 00:23:30.890
there is dedicated
support engineers

00:23:30.890 --> 00:23:34.610
looking at the metrics as Unity
goes live either with a game

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:36.770
or with a particular workload.

00:23:36.770 --> 00:23:39.200
Finally, the global
support operating model.

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:41.630
Our support team operates 24/7.

00:23:41.630 --> 00:23:45.710
Each and every Unity P1 case
is responded within 15 minutes,

00:23:45.710 --> 00:23:48.260
making sure that there
is somebody looking at it

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.270
every time something
critical happens.

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:52.790
Our support team is getting
more and more context-aware.

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:55.880
That means that they know the
architecture Unity is using.

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:59.270
They know the different
scenarios and the different use

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:01.580
cases Unity has so
that they can come

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:03.510
to a resolution much quicker.

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:06.620
This is done through creating
shared operational playbooks

00:24:06.620 --> 00:24:07.520
and customer context.

00:24:07.520 --> 00:24:09.670
And the TAMs are
playing a key role

00:24:09.670 --> 00:24:12.710
on making sure our support
team is completely aware

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:14.750
and has context on
what Unity is doing.

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:17.210
So all of these elements of
customer experience coming

00:24:17.210 --> 00:24:21.650
together, again, is key to
making Unity successful on GCP.

00:24:21.650 --> 00:24:24.990
The last piece that I'm
going to focus on now is--

00:24:24.990 --> 00:24:27.990
again, I talked about this
in the last two sections

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:31.040
already, which is training
and training at scale.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:33.200
Unity is a pretty
large organization.

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:35.270
They have hundreds of engineers.

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:37.950
And making sure
those engineers are--

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:41.250
they know GCP and skilled on
GCP is really, really important.

00:24:41.250 --> 00:24:44.660
So Google PSO is collaborating
constantly with Unity

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:47.510
and partners to deliver
at scale on the learning

00:24:47.510 --> 00:24:50.450
objectives of-- we have already
trained a hundred engineers.

00:24:50.450 --> 00:24:52.660
Our aim is to almost
double the number.

00:24:52.660 --> 00:24:54.830
We are identifying
the training needs.

00:24:54.830 --> 00:24:56.810
We are creating
customized courses

00:24:56.810 --> 00:24:59.480
to make sure they are fit
for use and fit for purpose

00:24:59.480 --> 00:25:01.400
for Unity's engineering org.

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:03.380
We are doing
multiple coordination

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:05.630
and scheduling across the
group because, again, they

00:25:05.630 --> 00:25:07.970
are very decentralized in
terms of the engineering

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:10.260
presence across the world.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:11.480
We are enabling sessions.

00:25:11.480 --> 00:25:13.550
And then we are also,
with sponsorship

00:25:13.550 --> 00:25:15.580
from our leadership,
have been enabling

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:17.960
the team to even get
certified on the platform.

00:25:17.960 --> 00:25:20.750
So we definitely trained
over a hundred engineers now.

00:25:20.750 --> 00:25:24.750
But we're also looking to double
the numbers in coming months.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:28.190
So all in all, I talked
about the different elements

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:30.470
behind the scene
that Google is doing

00:25:30.470 --> 00:25:32.300
to make sure that
Unity is getting

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:34.010
successful on the platform.

00:25:34.010 --> 00:25:35.780
Obviously, it has
taken a village

00:25:35.780 --> 00:25:38.630
to make the customer successful.

00:25:38.630 --> 00:25:41.750
These are all the team members
who have worked day in,

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:44.450
day out across the globe
in different regions,

00:25:44.450 --> 00:25:49.970
trying to make sure that Unity
scales up on GCP successfully

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:53.360
and can help its partner to
launch games on the platform

00:25:53.360 --> 00:25:55.790
successfully and
also overall have

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:57.330
a good operational experience.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:26:01.520
So again, all of them couldn't
be present in this session,

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:03.950
but I just wanted to call
out that it takes a village

00:26:03.950 --> 00:26:06.740
to support a large enterprise
account like Unity.

00:26:06.740 --> 00:26:09.110
So thank you for
everybody here who

00:26:09.110 --> 00:26:11.090
has successfully--
from Google side,

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:14.760
who was helped support Unity's
journey over the past two

00:26:14.760 --> 00:26:15.260
years.

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:17.890
So, massive, massive kudos.

00:26:17.890 --> 00:26:21.690
So that brings us to the end
of the presentation today.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:25.380
Thank you for listening
to me, Ananth, and Venk

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:27.450
as we went through
the Unity stories

00:26:27.450 --> 00:26:31.560
and how they have scaled up
and are managing operations

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:33.460
on the platform.

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:37.090
Thank you so much for joining
us today, and all the best.

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:38.780
Thank you.

